Java Fundamentals
1.1 What is Java?
Definition:

Java is a high-level, object-oriented, class-based programming language used to develop applications for web, mobile, desktop, enterprise systems and backend services.

Java follows the principle:

Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA).

It means you can compile a Java program into bytecode and execute that bytecode on different operating systems using a compatible JVM.
Real-world applications
Application	Java usage
Banking applications	Transactions and account management
E-commerce	Orders, payments and inventory
Enterprise software	Business applications
Backend development	REST APIs using Spring Boot
Android	Android application development
Microservices	Distributed backend systems

For example, imagine an online shopping application:
- Frontend displays products.
- Java backend processes orders.
- Database stores customer and product information.
- Payment service processes transactions.
Java can implement the backend logic that connects these components.
1.2 How Java works internally
You must understand this before learning Spring Boot.








dev.to


Java execution follows these steps:
1. Developer writes Java source code.
2. Source code is saved with .java extension.
3. Java compiler (javac) compiles the source code.
4. Compiler generates bytecode (.class).
5. JVM loads the bytecode.
6. JVM verifies and executes the bytecode.
7. The program produces output.
Example
Create a file called HelloWorld.java.
public class HelloWorld {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println("Hello Java!");

    }
}


Compilation:
javac HelloWorld.java


This generates:
HelloWorld.class


Execution:
java HelloWorld


Output:
Hello Java!


Important Java components
JDK – Java Development Kit

Complete development toolkit. It includes the compiler, development utilities and runtime components.

Real-world analogy: A complete workshop with all the tools needed to build something.


JRE – Java Runtime Environment

Provides the runtime environment and libraries needed to run Java applications.

Analogy: The environment required to operate the finished product.


JVM – Java Virtual Machine

Loads, verifies and executes Java bytecode.

Analogy: The engine that runs the Java application.


Remember:
- JDK = Development tools + runtime.
- JRE = Runtime environment.
- JVM = Executes bytecode.
Modern Java distributions may not provide a separate JRE installation, but the conceptual distinction remains important.
1.3 Variables in Java
Definition: A variable is a named memory location used to store data during program execution.

Real-world example:

A college student has:
- Name: Venu
- Age: 22
- Marks: 85.5
- Passed: true
We represent these values using variables.
Syntax
dataType variableName = value;


Implementation
public class VariablesExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String name = "Venu";
        int age = 22;
        double marks = 85.5;
        boolean passed = true;

        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(age);
        System.out.println(marks);
        System.out.println(passed);
    }
}


Output:
Venu
22
85.5
true


1.4 Data types in Java
Data types define what kind of value a variable can store.

Java has two major categories.

A. Primitive data types
Type	Example	Purpose
byte	byte a = 10;	Small integer
short	short a = 100;	Short integer
int	int a = 500;	Integer
long	long a = 10000L;	Large integer
float	float a = 10.5f;	Decimal
double	double a = 20.55;	More precision
char	char a = 'V';	Single character
boolean	boolean a = true;	True or false

B. Non-primitive/reference types
- String
- Arrays
- Classes
- Objects
- Interfaces
- Enums
Example:
String studentName = "Venu";

int[] marks = {80, 90, 85};


1.5 Operators
Operators perform operations on values and variables.

| Operator | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| + | Addition | 10 + 20 |
| - | Subtraction | 20 - 10 |
| * | Multiplication | 10 * 2 |
| / | Division | 20 / 2 |
| % | Remainder | 10 % 3 |
| == | Equality | a == b |
| != | Not equal | a != b |
| && | Logical AND | a > 5 && b > 5 |
| || | Logical OR | a > 5 || b > 5 |
Real-world implementation: Student eligibility
public class Eligibility {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int marks = 75;
        int attendance = 85;

        if (marks >= 40 && attendance >= 75) {

            System.out.println("Student is eligible");

        } else {

            System.out.println("Student is not eligible");

        }
    }
}


Execution flow:
1. Marks are 75.
2. Attendance is 85.
3. Check whether marks are greater than or equal to 40.
4. Check whether attendance is greater than or equal to 75.
5. Both conditions are true.
6. Print eligibility confirmation.
